//! regimen — grammar, v1
//!
//! This file is the normative description of the regimen format. A regimen is
//! a versioned, fixed combination of variables describing the character,
//! performance, and timing of a session. It is a deliberately minimal,
//! line-oriented subset of TOML: bare keys, scalar values, one level of
//! table, comments.
//!
//! Being a SUBSET of TOML is load-bearing, not decorative: `regimen.toml`
//! files under `results/` are read by this grammar and by Python's `tomllib`,
//! and the two must never disagree about the same file. So anything TOML
//! rejects, this grammar must reject too.
//!
//! v1 adds FLOATS and TABLES, because v0 could not spell an arm. Every arm a
//! public claim depends on carries a temperature and groups its settings, and
//! a regimen that cannot express a temperature is not a regimen -- v0's
//! restriction was scaffolding, not a position. Rendering `0.6` as the string
//! `"0.6"` would have been a lie about its type, and rendering it as a binary
//! float would have made `0.6` read back as `0.59999999999999998`, which for
//! a value that IDENTIFIES a regime is a different regime.
//!
//! Deliberately absent still, and expected to arrive when something needs
//! them: arrays, datetimes, nested tables, dotted keys, quoted keys,
//! multi-line strings, string escapes, exponents, `+` before a number,
//! leading zeros, and underscores in numbers.
//!
//! Three rules this grammar cannot state, which the parser therefore enforces:
//! a key may appear at most once in its own table, a table header may appear
//! at most once in a document, and an integer literal must fit in an `i64`.

file      =  { SOI ~ item* ~ EOI }

item      = _{ blank | table_header | statement | trailing }
blank     = _{ sp* ~ nl }
statement = _{ sp* ~ (pair ~ (sp* ~ comment)? | comment) ~ sp* ~ (nl | &EOI) }
trailing  = _{ sp+ ~ &EOI }

// `[sampler]` opens a table: every pair after it, up to the next header,
// belongs to it. One level and one bare key -- `[a.b]` is a dotted key
// wearing brackets, and dotted keys are still absent.
//
// The rule admits ANY table name rather than the three the arms use today
// (`sampler`, `limits`, `triggers`), for the same reason `key` admits any
// bare key rather than a list of the fields a regimen happens to carry: a
// grammar states shape, and a vocabulary in a grammar is a vocabulary nobody
// can extend without editing the normative file.
table_header = { sp* ~ "[" ~ sp* ~ key ~ sp* ~ "]" ~ sp* ~ comment? ~ (nl | &EOI) }

pair      =  { key ~ sp* ~ "=" ~ sp* ~ value }
key       =  { (ASCII_ALPHANUMERIC | "_" | "-")+ }

// `float` before `integer`: PEG choice is ordered and does not re-enter, so
// `integer` would take the `0` of `0.6` and strand `.6` for the statement
// rule to choke on.
value     = _{ string | boolean | float | integer }
string    =  { "\"" ~ inner ~ "\"" }
inner     =  { char* }
// Two rules rather than one with two spellings, so that the parser matches on
// the RULE rather than on the text. A match arm on a string literal compiles
// fine when a third spelling is added and quietly stops covering it; a match
// on a rule is a match on a generated enum, and the new variant has nowhere to
// hide. scripts/check-library.py refuses the string form outright.
boolean   = _{ boolean_true | boolean_false }
boolean_true  = { "true" }
boolean_false = { "false" }

// No leading zeros and no leading `+`, because TOML admits neither. `-0` is
// legal TOML and so is legal here.
integer   =  { "-"? ~ ("0" | (ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT*)) }

// A float is an exact DECIMAL: the digits as written, never a binary float.
// This rule is character-for-character the `decimal` rule of the record's
// grammar, and deliberately so -- `formats::record::json::Decimal::new` is
// what this module builds the value with, and that constructor checks the
// text against the RECORD's rule. Two rules that must agree, one of which
// does the enforcing: if they ever drift, a document this grammar accepts
// fails to build a value and the parser says so, rather than a regimen and a
// record disagreeing about the same digits in silence. A test drives a table
// of literals through both readers.
//
// `-0.0` is refused, as it is in the record: one spelling per value, and
// negative zero is a second spelling of zero. (`-0` as an INTEGER is
// accepted, because it was before this rule existed and TOML admits it; that
// asymmetry is real and is the one thing here a later version should settle.)
//
// No exponent. `7e-1` is a second spelling of `0.7`, and a regime identified
// by its settings cannot afford two ways to write one setting.
float     = @{ ("-" ~ negative_float) | (int_part ~ "." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+) }
negative_float = _{
    (nonzero ~ "." ~ ASCII_DIGIT+) | ("0" ~ "." ~ "0"* ~ ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT*)
}
int_part  = _{ "0" | nonzero }
nonzero   = _{ ASCII_NONZERO_DIGIT ~ ASCII_DIGIT* }

comment   =  { "#" ~ (!(nl | control) ~ ANY)* }

// A raw control character is forbidden inside a string, as in TOML. Tab is
// not a control character for this purpose; a line break is, which is what
// stops a string spanning lines.
char      = _{ !("\"" | "\\" | control) ~ ANY }
control   = _{ '\u{0000}'..'\u{0008}' | '\u{000A}'..'\u{001F}' | "\u{007F}" }

// The line terminator is stated rather than inherited. pest's built-in
// NEWLINE also matches a bare carriage return, which TOML does not accept,
// and a CR-only document would otherwise pass this grammar while `tomllib`
// rejected the very same bytes.
nl        = _{ "\r\n" | "\n" }
sp        = _{ " " | "\t" }
